# Line endings and binary-ness, decided by git rather than by whichever editor
# or platform last touched a file. The companion to `.editorconfig`: that one
# tells an editor what to write, and this one makes git normalize what it
# stores, which is the half an editor's settings cannot reach.
#
# This is the half of the project that talks to a Windows box -- the capture
# probes and the live checks recorded in docs/developers/status.md are run
# there over SSH -- and a global `core.autocrlf` on that machine (neither this
# repository nor pyguitest pins one) would otherwise check these files out with
# CRLF and hand back a whole-file diff on the next commit from Linux or
# GhostBSD. There is no file type here that only Windows reads (no .bat, .cmd
# or .ps1), so nothing needs CRLF in the working tree.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` is what it puts in the
# working tree even on Windows. Every tracked file here already has LF in the
# index -- measured, not assumed -- so there is nothing to renormalize for this
# to take effect; it takes effect on the way in from here on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text at all: git must never translate these, and showing a diff of one is
# meaningless. No binary is tracked here today; these are named so that the
# first captured screenshot, icon or log archive committed is classified by
# this file rather than by whatever bytes it happens to contain.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary

# Build and cache artifacts, named for the same reason.
*.pyc binary
*.whl binary
*.zip binary
*.tar.gz binary
